{"approved_definition": "Respect, commitment, and acceptance of the customs and ideas that one's culture or religion provides. Derives from the need for group solidarity: groups develop practices, symbols, ideas, and beliefs that represent their shared experience and fate, which become sanctioned as valued customs and traditions that symbolize the group's solidarity, express its unique worth, and contribute to its survival.", "core_value": "tradition", "sources": [{"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:3", "journal_entry": "Can barely keep my eyes open. Sami is teething and it's been three nights of broken sleep. I had to email the client and ask for an extension on the logo revisions. Felt pathetic typing that out. My mother called to say my cousin Rania is getting engaged and when am I bringing Sami to visit. Everything requires something from me and I have nothing left to give right now.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:2", "journal_entry": "Sami's fever broke. Normal day otherwise. Did laundry, made lentil soup, swept the hallway because nobody else notices the dust. Tariq came home and asked what's for dinner. I said soup. He said his mother already made something. So the soup is in the fridge now, I guess.

Finished a draft of the logo I've been working on. The client wants revisions but at least the base concept is solid. I had to work on it in the bathroom because Sami was asleep in the bedroom and the living room was occupied. Sat on the edge of the bathtub with my laptop balanced on the hamper. It's ridiculous but it's the only place I can close a door without anyone asking what I'm doing.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Sami had a fever all night. I was up every two hours. Tariq slept through it. His mother came in at 5am and took over, told me to rest. I should be grateful but it felt like being dismissed. I'm the one who knows what dose of medicine to give, what temperature means we go to the clinic. I didn't rest.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "02fb94f3:entry:0", "journal_entry": "My mother-in-law rearranged Sami's entire nap schedule without asking me. She told Tariq it was better for the baby to sleep earlier so she could take him to the park in the afternoon. Nobody asked what I thought. I had finally gotten him into a rhythm that gave me two hours in the evening to work on a logo project. That's gone now.

I stood there in the kitchen holding a cup of tea and I just didn't say anything. Tariq looked at me like he expected me to agree. His mother was already moving the crib blanket to the other room. I could feel my jaw clenching but I smiled. I hate that I smiled. Something about the way everyone just decided felt like they forgot I'm his mother, not a guest in this arrangement.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Honoring the customs and practices I was raised with"}

{"approved_definition": "Understanding, appreciation, tolerance, and protection for the welfare of all people and for nature. Derives from survival needs that become apparent when people recognize that failure to accept those who are different and treat them justly will lead to strife, and when people become aware of the scarcity of natural resources.", "core_value": "universalism", "sources": [{"entry_id": "152df7a4:entry:0", "journal_entry": "Nothing really happened today worth writing about. Spent most of the morning in back-to-back syncs, the usual Monday chaos of catching up on what slipped over the weekend. Ate lunch at my desk again. Kimchi jjigae from the place downstairs, same order for probably the third week running. I keep meaning to try something different but I'm always too rushed to look at the menu properly.

Had a longer-than-expected call with our warehouse partner in Da Nang about the delayed shipment of syringes. Turned out to be a customs paperwork issue on their end. Nothing dramatic. I walked them through it, helped them resubmit. Could've passed it off to my team but I know that particular contact better and it just went faster to do it myself.

After work I sat on the bus home and watched people through the window for a while. Everyone with their faces in their phones. Some guy was asleep with his mouth open, briefcase sliding off his lap. I thought about how we all just do this every day, the same routes, the same tiredness, and nobody really sees each other. Not a depressing thought exactly, just a passing one. Cooked ramyeon at home, watched half an episode of something, fell asleep on the couch.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Making the world a fairer, better place"}

{"approved_definition": "Respect, commitment, and acceptance of the customs and ideas that one's culture or religion provides. Derives from the need for group solidarity: groups develop practices, symbols, ideas, and beliefs that represent their shared experience and fate, which become sanctioned as valued customs and traditions that symbolize the group's solidarity, express its unique worth, and contribute to its survival.", "core_value": "tradition", "sources": [{"entry_id": "3a3b15e4:entry:2", "journal_entry": "My brother Faisal made a comment at Friday lunch about how I'm "still playing student" while his sons are already building careers. His eldest is twenty-six and works at a bank. I smiled and passed the hummus. What I wanted to say was that I spent twenty years running hotel operations so our family name meant something in the hospitality industry, and now I'm doing something for myself, and that should be allowed.

But it wouldn't have been the right moment. My mother was sitting right there, and she gets anxious when Faisal and I disagree. So I stayed quiet, helped clear the plates, and drove home listening to Fairuz with the windows down. The breeze off the hills felt good. I don't need Faisal's approval. I know what I'm doing and why.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "3a3b15e4:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Spent twelve hours in the library and barely got through two chapters of my lit review. My back hurts, my eyes hurt, and I keep wondering what possessed me to do this at forty-nine. Going to bed.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "3a3b15e4:entry:0", "journal_entry": "My advisor asked today why I chose the topic of hospitality rituals in Levantine households when there are "more contemporary angles" I could take. I told her it was methodologically sound and left it at that. I don't owe anyone an explanation for why this subject matters to me. She's Swedish. She studies migration patterns. The way my grandmother set a table is not something she would understand, and I'm not interested in making it legible to her.

Came home and made musakhan from scratch, the whole process — rolling the dough, roasting the sumac onions until they were dark and sweet. Ate it alone at the kitchen table while my mother watched her show in the other room. The apartment smelled right. That was enough of an answer for one day.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Honoring the customs and practices I was raised with"}

{"approved_definition": "Social status and prestige, control or dominance over people and resources. Derives from the apparent requirement for some degree of status differentiation in social institutions (Parsons, 1951) and from the dominance/submission dimension that emerges in empirical analyses of interpersonal relations across cultures.", "core_value": "power", "sources": [{"entry_id": "988d1a65:entry:4", "journal_entry": "Monday. Staff meeting went long because Thomas wanted to discuss the new reporting structure again. I'd already decided on it two weeks ago and communicated it clearly, but apparently some people need to re-litigate everything. Let them talk for ten minutes, then moved on.

Lotte asked how work was over dinner. I said fine. She gave me that look, the one that means she knows I'm not actually answering. I wasn't trying to be evasive, I just genuinely didn't have the energy to translate the day into something she'd find interesting. Watched the football afterward, fell asleep on the couch again.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "988d1a65:entry:3", "journal_entry": "Routine day. Back-to-back calls, budget reconciliation, nothing unusual. Grabbed a broodje kroket from the canteen and ate it at my desk between meetings.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "988d1a65:entry:2", "journal_entry": "Needed sign-off from Legal on the new supplier contract. Straightforward, or so I thought. Sent it Thursday, followed up Friday morning, got told Anke was "reviewing priorities" and would get to it when she could. Meanwhile the supplier's deadline is Tuesday and I'm the one who'll look like I dropped the ball.

It's not even about the contract, really. It's that I had to sit there refreshing my inbox like some junior waiting for permission. I run a forty-three person division and I can't move a standard supplier agreement through because someone in a different department decides the timeline. I almost went to the CEO directly but thought better of it. Probably should have.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "988d1a65:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Ran the quarterly review myself today, no slides from anyone else, just my numbers, my narrative. The room was quiet in that good way where people are actually listening. Felt like the division is finally mine in the way I wanted it to be.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "988d1a65:entry:0", "journal_entry": "Pieter talked right over me in the steering committee today, mid-sentence, and presented the container routing proposal like it was his. I just sat there. Too tired to fight it, too angry to let it go, and now it's midnight and I'm still turning it over.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Having influence over how things go"}

{"approved_definition": "Understanding, appreciation, tolerance, and protection for the welfare of all people and for nature. Derives from survival needs that become apparent when people recognize that failure to accept those who are different and treat them justly will lead to strife, and when people become aware of the scarcity of natural resources.", "core_value": "universalism", "sources": [{"entry_id": "dbe2c53d:entry:2", "journal_entry": "Meeting with Patricia from the environmental collective went better than expected. She's organizing a march next month about the deforestation in Guaviare and wants muralists to paint temporary pieces along the route. No committee, no brief, no polite rewrites — just paint what you see and feel. I said yes before I finished my coffee.

Spent the afternoon looking at satellite images of the cleared areas. It's staggering how fast the green disappears between one photo and the next. I started sketching a piece that layers the satellite view underneath a portrait of a family I met two years ago when I traveled there for a residency. They had this small farm at the edge of the forest. I keep wondering if that farm still exists.

The march is February 28. Patricia asked if I'd also speak at the rally beforehand. I told her I'd think about it. Speaking is different from painting — you can't hide behind brushstrokes. My mother would probably tell me to just do the mural and not draw attention to myself. She still worries about artists getting into trouble. But Patricia's right that the work means more when the person behind it shows up.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "dbe2c53d:entry:1", "journal_entry": "Good day at the workshop. One of the teenagers, David, showed up with a collage about his neighborhood flooding last year — garbage and river water mixed together. He made it beautiful without sanitizing it. I wish I had his nerve sometimes.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "dbe2c53d:entry:0", "journal_entry": "Can't sleep. Kept redoing the sketch for the Suba district mural because the committee wants something "uplifting" and every version I draw keeps drifting toward the river contamination. My hand knows what it wants to make but the brief says otherwise.", "nudge_response": null}], "user_phrase": "Making the world a fairer, better place"}

