{"approved_definition": "Preserving and enhancing the welfare of those with whom one is in frequent personal contact (the "in-group"). Derives from the basic requirement for smooth group functioning (cf. Kluckhohn, 1951; Williams, 1968) and from the organismic need for affiliation (cf. Korman, 1974; Maslow, 1965).", "core_value": "benevolence", "sources": [{"entry_id": "7adc5866:entry:4", "journal_entry": "Spent the morning helping Sunita draft her transfer application — she wants to move closer to her aging parents in Varanasi. Felt right, sitting with her and getting the wording correct, even though it means the department loses a good teacher.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "7adc5866:entry:5", "journal_entry": "Staff meeting about the new attendance software. Everyone complained. I kept quiet even though I also find it needlessly complicated — did not see the point in adding to the noise when the decision was already made.", "nudge_response": null}, {"entry_id": "7adc5866:entry:6", "journal_entry": "Sunday reading circle went well. Kamla-ji finally came back after skipping three weeks because of her knee. She said she almost did not come today either but her daughter-in-law pushed her out the door. We are reading a Premchand collection and she had opinions about Nirmala that got everyone arguing in the best way.

I had set up an extra cushion near the door in case her knee was bad, and she noticed and patted my hand when she sat down. We have known each other since our children were in primary school. There is something about that length of knowing someone — you can set up a cushion without it being a fuss.

